STATEMENT 3: InsertProductAsync
================================================================================
DMS Conversion Timestamp: 2026-02-19T16:51:02.616920
DMS Status: error
DMS Error: Metadata model creation failed: {'error': 'Unknown metadata model creation status: RECEIVED'}

Original SQL Server Statement:
DECLARE @NewProductId INT;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
    VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity);
    
    SET @NewProductId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
    
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (@NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, GETDATE());
    
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
        LastUpdated = GETDATE()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
COMMIT;

SELECT @NewProductId;

Conversion Approach: 
- Convert DECLARE @variable to DO $$ DECLARE variable or use RETURNING clause
- Replace SCOPE_IDENTITY() with RETURNING clause on INSERT
- Replace GETDATE() with NOW() or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
- BEGIN TRANSACTION becomes BEGIN in PostgreSQL
- COMMIT remains COMMIT
However, for ADO.NET usage, transactions are handled at connection level.
Restructure to use RETURNING for the inserted ID directly.

